  "result": "I added `split(cents: int, parts: int) -> list[int]` to `shop/money.py`, and the full test suite passes: 13 tests, `OK`.\n\n- **Behaviour:** each element starts at `cents // parts`, and the leftover cents go one at a time to the first elements. So `split(10, 3)` returns `[4, 3, 3]`. If `parts` is below 1 it raises `ValueError`.\n- **Tests:** there was no money test file, so I created `tests/test_money.py` in the same `unittest` style as `tests/test_pricing.py`. It covers an even split, leftover cents going to the front, the total matching `cents`, `parts=1`, `split(0, 2)`, and `ValueError` for zero and negative `parts`.\n- **How I ran them:** `python3 -m unittest discover -s tests -t .`\n\n**Decision for you: negative amounts.** I didn't test them, and they don't follow the \"first element gets the extra\" rule. `split(-10, 3)` returns `[-3, -3, -4]`: the total is right, but the extra cent goes to the last element. If you want `[-4, -3, -3]`, I can split the absolute value and flip the sign of each result.\n\nNothing is committed.",
